.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt PAZARI-STRESS 1
.Os pazari
.Sh NAME
.Nm pazari-stress
.Nd deterministic memory workload generator
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -interval Ar duration
.Op Fl -arena Ar size
.Op Fl -no-reserve
.Op Fl -no-thp
.Op Fl -fill Cm index | random
.Ar phase ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
touches memory to a written plan and reports what it did, one JSON object per
line, on standard output.
It is meant for tests: the load has a shape that is known in advance, and the
record of what the load did is a file rather than a graph.
.Pp
The tool holds exactly one mapping, the
.Em arena :
anonymous, private, readable and writable, sized once at startup and never
resized.
Inside the arena it keeps a
.Em frontier ,
the byte offset up to which pages have been touched.
Every phase of the plan moves the frontier, or deliberately does not:
.Bl -tag -width "release" -offset indent
.It Cm grow
Advances the frontier, writing into pages the run has not touched since they
were last dropped.
Each such write is a page fault.
.It Cm release
Retreats the frontier, dropping the pages behind it with
.Dv MADV_DONTNEED .
.It Cm churn
Rewrites what is already resident without moving the frontier.
.It Cm hold
Touches nothing.
.El
.Pp
A
.Cm grow
that advances over pages an earlier
.Cm release
dropped faults them in again.
.Pp
The run is single-threaded and synchronous.
It makes no allocation the plan did not ask for.
A phase that reports no progress is therefore one the kernel is not letting
proceed.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -interval Ar duration
How often a running phase reports its progress.
The default is
.Cm 500ms .
Parsed as a free-form duration, so
.Cm 250ms ,
.Cm 2s
and
.Cm 1m 30s
are all accepted.
.It Fl -arena Ar size
How much address space to map.
The default is the plan's own peak frontier, which is the smallest arena the
plan can run in.
Name a larger one to map more than the plan will touch; a plan that never
grows has no peak of its own and must be given an arena.
A plan whose frontier would run past the arena is refused before the mapping
is made.
.It Fl -no-reserve
Map the arena
.Dv MAP_NORESERVE ,
so that it is never committed up front.
Without this the whole arena is charged to
.Va Committed_AS
at
.Xr mmap 2
time, and under strict overcommit an arena larger than memory plus swap
cannot be mapped at all.
With it, nothing is charged until a page is touched.
Either way the cgroup the agent brakes only ever sees the pages the tool has
actually written.
.It Fl -no-thp
Apply
.Dv MADV_NOHUGEPAGE
to the arena, keeping transparent huge pages out of it.
Without this, a guest on
.Va transparent_hugepage=always
faults the arena in 2MiB at a time, and a workload that overshoots
.Va memory.high
by a huge page overshoots it by 512 times what the brake expected.
.It Fl -fill Cm index | random
What every touched page is filled with.
The default is
.Cm index .
See
.Sx FILL PATTERNS .
.El
.Sh PHASES
A phase spec is a kind, an
.Ql = ,
its target, and then comma-separated
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
options:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
grow=2GiB[,rate=100MiB/s][,chunk=1MiB]
churn=30s,rate=500MiB/s[,chunk=1MiB]
hold=30s
release=1GiB[,rate=100MiB/s][,chunk=64MiB]
.Ed
.Pp
Phases run in the order they are given.
.Bl -tag -width "release" -offset indent
.It Cm grow Ns = Ns Ar size
Advances the frontier by
.Ar size .
.Cm rate
is optional; the default
.Cm chunk
is
.Cm 1MiB .
.It Cm churn Ns = Ns Ar duration
Rewrites the resident set for
.Ar duration .
.Cm rate
is required, because a churn has no other end to reach; the default
.Cm chunk
is
.Cm 1MiB .
.It Cm hold Ns = Ns Ar duration
Waits
.Ar duration ,
touching nothing.
Takes no options.
.It Cm release Ns = Ns Ar size
Retreats the frontier by
.Ar size .
.Cm rate
is optional; the default
.Cm chunk
is
.Cm 64MiB .
.El
.Pp
.Cm rate
is a byte count per second, written with a
.Ql /s
suffix.
.Cm chunk
is how much memory one pass of the inner loop moves before the pacer is
consulted again; a smaller chunk paces more evenly and costs more overhead.
.Pp
Byte counts are parsed with both scales: the binary suffixes
.Cm KiB , MiB , GiB
and
.Cm TiB
count 1024, and the decimal suffixes
.Cm kB , MB , GB
and
.Cm TB
count 1000.
A bare number is a count of bytes.
Zero is never a legal target, rate, chunk or arena.
.Pp
The whole plan is parsed and checked before anything is mapped.
A release that would retreat past the start of the arena, a churn over an
arena nothing has grown into yet, and a plan that reaches past the arena are
all refused at that point, naming the phase by its index.
.Ss Page granularity
The frontier is a byte offset, but memory moves in pages.
.Pp
A
.Cm grow
faults in every page its range reaches, including a page the range only ends
inside.
A
.Cm release
drops only the pages its range holds whole: half a page cannot go back while
the other half is still live, so a range that starts or ends inside a page
leaves that page resident.
.Sh FILL PATTERNS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm index
One word at the start of the page, derived from the page's own ordinal.
Cheap, and every page ends up distinct.
.It Cm random
The whole page filled with inline xorshift output, seeded from the page's own
ordinal.
Incompressible, so a zram device cannot hold the page for less than it costs.
Use it wherever a compressed swap device would otherwise make the guest look
roomier than it is.
.El
.Sh OUTPUT
The tool writes its events to standard output and its logs to standard error,
so a caller can redirect the stream to a file and still see what went wrong.
.Pp
Each event is one JSON object on one line, carrying a
.Va type
member naming the event in
.Cm snake_case .
Memory quantities are counts of bytes as JSON integers in the
.Vt u64
range.
.Va elapsed_ms
is milliseconds since the process started, taken from a monotonic clock.
.Pp
An event stream is exactly one
.Cm started ,
then for each phase in order one
.Cm phase ,
zero or more
.Cm progress ,
and one
.Cm completed ,
and then one terminal event.
An interruption or a failure replaces the rest of the stream.
.Ss started
The first event.
.Bl -tag -width "no_reserve" -offset indent
.It Va pid
The workload's process id.
.It Va page_size
The guest's page size.
.It Va arena
What was mapped, rounded up to a whole page.
.It Va no_reserve
Whether the arena was mapped
.Dv MAP_NORESERVE .
.It Va no_thp
Whether huge pages were kept out of the arena.
.It Va fill
.Cm index
or
.Cm random .
.It Va plan
The parsed plan, one object per phase, in the order it runs.
Each entry carries the
.Va spec
it was parsed from, its
.Va kind ,
its
.Va target_bytes
or
.Va target_ms ,
its
.Va chunk ,
and its
.Va rate_bps
if it has one.
.El
.Ss phase
Emitted once as a phase starts.
Exactly one of
.Va target_bytes
and
.Va target_ms
is present, decided by
.Va kind .
.Bl -tag -width "target_bytes" -offset indent
.It Va index
The phase's position in the plan, from zero.
.It Va kind
.Cm grow , churn , hold
or
.Cm release .
.It Va target_bytes
What a
.Cm grow
or a
.Cm release
moves.
.It Va target_ms
How long a
.Cm churn
or a
.Cm hold
runs.
.El
.Ss progress
Emitted every
.Fl -interval
while a phase runs, for every kind of phase.
A phase that ends before its first interval is due reports no
.Cm progress
at all.
.Bl -tag -width "elapsed_ms" -offset indent
.It Va index
The running phase.
.It Va elapsed_ms
Since the process started.
.It Va touched
What this phase has moved so far: bytes written for a
.Cm grow
and a
.Cm churn ,
bytes released for a
.Cm release ,
and zero for a
.Cm hold .
It resets at the start of every phase.
.It Va frontier
Where the frontier stands now.
.It Va rate_bps
What was moved since the last
.Cm progress ,
per second.
This is the number that falls when the brake catches the workload.
.El
.Ss completed
Emitted once a phase reaches its target.
Carries
.Va index
and
.Va elapsed_ms .
.Ss done
The last event of a run that finished its plan.
Carries
.Va elapsed_ms .
.Ss interrupted
The last event of a run stopped by
.Dv SIGTERM
or
.Dv SIGINT .
Carries
.Va index ,
the phase that was running, and
.Va elapsed_ms .
.Ss failed
The last event of a run that could not carry on.
Carries
.Va elapsed_ms
and
.Va error ,
a human-readable string for logs.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev RUST_LOG
The log filter for standard error, in the directive syntax the
.Va tracing
crate's environment filter reads.
The default is
.Cm info .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
The run ended with
.Cm done
or with
.Cm interrupted .
.It 1
The run ended with
.Cm failed .
.It 2
The command line itself was refused, before any event was written.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Grow two gigabytes at a paced rate, sit on them, and hand most of them back:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pazari-stress --no-reserve \e
    grow=2GiB,rate=384MiB/s hold=30s release=1536MiB hold=60s
.Ed
.Pp
Burst as fast as the kernel will allow, which is what a brake has to survive:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pazari-stress --arena 4GiB grow=200MiB,rate=200MiB/s grow=3GiB hold=300s
.Ed
.Pp
Fill with incompressible pages, so that a zram device cannot hide the cost:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pazari-stress --fill random --no-thp grow=1GiB,rate=128MiB/s hold=120s
.Ed
.Pp
Read the stream back as it is written:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pazari-stress grow=1GiB hold=10s | jq -c 'select(.type == "progress")'
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pazari-agent 1 ,
.Xr pazarid 1 ,
.Xr pazari 7
.Pp
.Xr madvise 2 ,
.Xr mmap 2
